f ℳf\\left(z\\right) is usually an infinite strip a<\\mathrm{\\Re }z<b parallel to the imaginary axis. The inversion formula is given by\nL62: \nL63: 2.5.2  |  | f(t)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\mathrm{i}}{\\int }_{c-\\mathrm{i}\\mathrm{\\infty }}^{c+\\mathrm{i}\\mathrm{\\infty }}{t}^{-z}ℳf\\left(z\\right)dz,  |\nL64: ⓘ\nL65: Symbols:\nL66:     cite34†ℳ\\left(f\\right)\\left(s\\right): Mellin transform , cite42†\\pi : the ratio of the circumference of a circle to its diameter , cite35†dx: differential of x , cite43†\\mathrm{i}: imaginary unit , cite35†\\int : integral , cite36†f(x): locally integrable function and cite36†c: point L67: Keywords:\nL68:     cite21†Mellin transform L69: Permalink:\nL70:     cite44†http://dlmf.nist.gov/2.5.E2 L71: Encodings:\nL72:     cite45†TeX , cite46†pMML , cite47†png L73: Notational Change (effective with 1.0.15):\nL74:     The notation for the Mellin transform was changed to ℳf\\left(z\\right) from ℳ(f;z).\nL75: See also:\nL76:     Annotations for cite41†§2.5(i) , cite33†§2.5 and cite17†Ch.2 L77: with a<c<b.\nL78: \nL79: One of the two convolution integrals associated with the Mellin transform is of the form\nL80: \nL81: 2.5.3  |  | I(x)={\\int }_{0}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}f(t)h(xt)dt,  |\nL82: x>0,\nL83: ⓘ\nL84: Symbols:\nL85:     cite35†dx: differential of x , cite35†\\int : integral , cite36†f(x): locally integrable function , cite48†I(x): convolution integral and cite48†h(x): function L86: Referenced by:\nL87:     cite31†§2.5(ii) , cite49†§2.5(ii) L88: Permalink:\nL89:     cite50†http://dlmf.nist.gov/2.5.E3 L90: Encodings:\nL91:     cite51†TeX , cite52†pMML , cite53†png L92: See also:\nL93:     Annotations for cite41†§2.5(i) , cite33†§2.5 and cite17†Ch.2 L94: \nL95: and\nL96: \nL97: 2.5.4  |  | ℳI\\left(z\\right)=ℳf\\left(1-z\\right)ℳh\\left(z\\right).  |\nL98: ⓘ\nL99: Symbols:\nL100:     cite34†ℳ\\left(f\\right)\\left(s\\right): Mellin transform , ci